00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:15.493
Let's get started.

00:03:15.493 --> 00:03:17.910
JOHN BOHANNON: OK, I'm going
to do an experiment with you.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:20.520
So everyone, do me a favor
and close your laptops,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.680
at least for five minutes.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.390
Thank you.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.130
All right, ready?

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:30.060
If you've done this experiment
before, if this looks familiar,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.320
keep it to yourself.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.060
Don't spoil the others.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.890
It's a very powerful
effect when it works,

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:38.320
so I want everyone to
have this experience.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:39.030
OK, here we go.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:43.290
Ready?

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.120
A boy and his father
are in a car crash.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.280
The father is killed instantly.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.977
The boy is rushed
to the hospital

00:03:54.977 --> 00:03:56.810
and prepped for surgery
with one of the best

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:58.460
surgeons in the country.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:03:59.920
Lucky him.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.680
Doctor arrives, sees the
boy on the table, and says,

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.090
I can't operate on this patient.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:07.140
He's my son.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.960
Now, when I first saw this
myself, a couple of years back,

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.290
I was absolutely stumped.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:17.550
Like, my mind just raced
to explain the paradox.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.529
I'm sure right
now in your minds,

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:21.990
you're coming up with
explanations for this.

00:04:24.732 --> 00:04:28.822
So, who is the surgeon?

00:04:28.822 --> 00:04:31.180
Michelle?

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.950
MICHELLE CASBON: There's a
simple explanation for this.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:35.553
It's the mother.

00:04:35.553 --> 00:04:37.220
JOHN BOHANNON: Raise
your hand if you've

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:39.262
seen this for the first
time, and you immediately

00:04:39.262 --> 00:04:41.980
knew the mother was the answer.

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:47.080
One, two, three.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:49.110
Three, OK.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.710
That's a little bit
lower than average.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.660
Typically, 85% of
people are stumped

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.960
by this, which means, I guess,
by extension, more than three

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.270
out of this room
typically get it.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:01.690
But don't feel bad.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:03.240
I was also totally
stumped by it.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.180
Michelle was totally
stumped by it.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.440
You are just a good
learning machine.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.090
You've been trained
your entire life

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.950
on a stream of
very, very good data

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:19.820
that supports the prior
that surgeons are men.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:22.070
So that's cognitive bias.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.830
The reason that felt
hard was because you

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:25.800
have cognitive bias.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:26.820
We all do.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:28.840
And so, the goal--

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.590
and we're going to
describe one of these--

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.290
is to find technologies
that can help

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:38.370
us identify these biases that we
have and help us reverse them.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:44.060
So here's a system that's
built on human decisions.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:48.350
So Wikipedia is an example
of a web scale system, where

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:51.090
it's really just amplifying
the individual decisions

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.270
of individual humans.

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:55.820
So what happens is all
those cognitive biases

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:59.330
in those brains get inherited
by the web scale system.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.330
You just can't avoid it.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.360
It ends up happening.

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:04.710
Let me give you some examples.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.850
But before I do, I
just want to emphasize

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.670
the astounding thing
about Wikipedia

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.990
is that it works at all.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.590
So I'm going to be pointing
out some well-known problems

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:16.550
with Wikipedia.

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:19.250
But that's by no
means a deal-breaker.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:22.650
I use it every day, I'm sure
like most of you do as well.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:27.140
It is the world's most important
first point of information

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:28.760
about most topics.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:32.100
And yet, there's absolutely no
one in charge of this thing.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.460
It's really just a big
volunteer effort-- millions

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.400
of people deliberating
with each other

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:42.210
to reach consensus on facts
about the world worth knowing.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.225
So let me give you an example.

00:06:44.225 --> 00:06:46.100
So if you've never heard
of Donna Strickland,

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:49.470
I hope that you'll
never forget her now.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:52.910
So last year, she won the Nobel
Prize in physics for her work

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:54.620
on laser physics.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:58.670
First woman to win this prize
in 55 years-- it's a big deal.

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:01.100
On the day it was
announced, the world

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.910
was astounded to discover that
she had no Wikipedia page.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:08.080
She now has a very long,
very detailed Wikipedia page.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.220
I can't even capture
the entire table

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:13.320
of contents on the screenshot.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.770
You may be surprised to learn
that all that information

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:18.680
that you can now learn
about this person

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.090
did not appear overnight.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.090
It is not the case that
she won the Nobel Prize,

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.130
and suddenly, there was
all this information

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:28.010
available that was really
significant and notable that

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:29.430
deserved a Wikipedia page.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:31.100
It was always there.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.560
Somehow, we just missed it.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:36.920
Let me also point out
that on that very day,

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.020
this fellow won the
same prize with her.

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:41.180
This is her co-laureate.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:44.570
He has a much less
detailed page.

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:49.320
But it has existed since 2004.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:53.700
Let me show you an example of
a typical web page, a Wikipedia

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:55.080
page about a scientist.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.470
I found this guy at
random, just using my data.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:02.130
Totally typical meat and
potatoes Wikipedia page

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:04.590
about a scientist--

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:08.940
is based at some
research institute,

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:12.090
studies a certain topic,
and has some public impact.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:15.600
There are citable,
reputable news sources

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.880
about this person and his work.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:19.710
And so you get a Wikipedia page.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:22.920
Totally normal, nothing
controversial here.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:27.330
Now, in the same query where
I found him in my data,

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:29.490
I found a colleague
of his, who works

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.030
on almost the same thing--

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:34.289
very similar topic,
public health.

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:37.679
There's actually more to say
about this colleague of his.

00:08:41.059 --> 00:08:44.360
I would love to show it
to you, except it's pretty

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:46.850
hard to fit it on the screen.

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.390
Her name is Ami Zota.

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.360
And the reason it just
won't fit on the screen

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.860
is because there's
this huge warning

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:54.850
banner stuck on her page.

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:58.770
This is basically
the process by which

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.140
Wikipedia pages get deleted.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:02.963
So the community basically--

00:09:02.963 --> 00:09:04.380
first, it starts
in the talk page,

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.930
where they say this person
just isn't that notable.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.910
Then someone decides
to slap a big banner,

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:12.720
and eventually, essentially,
this gets fixed,

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:15.260
or the page gets taken down.

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:18.580
So there's no bad guy here.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:21.070
There's no evil
group of people out

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:24.130
to delete scientists
from Wikipedia.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.320
These are good
citizens just trying

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.390
to keep Wikipedia's
standards high.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:32.470
But the problem is, it's
a subjective decision.

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:35.260
How do you decide
whose page is not

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:39.220
worthy of the world knowing?

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:40.880
You just have to use your gut.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.270
And that's just an invitation
for your cognitive biases

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.950
to have an effect.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:51.072
So I also want to show you an
example of the opposite effect.

00:09:51.072 --> 00:09:52.780
How many of you have
ever heard of Sting?

00:09:55.330 --> 00:10:01.620
Now, take your hand down if
that Sting was the singer.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.132
Anyone still have their hand up?

00:10:03.132 --> 00:10:04.608
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:10:04.608 --> 00:10:06.750
JOHN BOHANNON: Oh, very strong.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:07.380
Very strong.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:07.980
OK.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:11.520
I want you to keep that in mind.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:15.150
But here's an example
of a scientist

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:20.250
who has the longest and most
detailed Wikipedia page of all.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:24.660
So there is no female scientist
who even comes close to this.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:26.910
And it's not because it's
the most important scientist

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:28.743
in the world, or her
work is that important.

00:10:28.743 --> 00:10:35.530
It's because her life intersects
with a topic that people just

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:37.330
pay a huge amount
of attention to,

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:39.700
namely the post
9/11 war on terror.

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:45.010
So this is just to show you
that overattention can also

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:47.410
be a result of cognitive bias.

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:51.880
Now, here's your favorite Sting.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:55.210
Her page, the one I just showed
you, was 10,000 words long.

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:57.990
This one's 20,000 words long.

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:02.110
And in fact, of all the
occupations of people,

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:08.410
the occupations that have by far
the most attention about whom

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:13.840
the most details are kept on
Wikipedia, are updated the most

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:17.680
zealously, so that every
little micro detail that you

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:23.170
can update them on gets updated,
are definitely not scientists.

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:24.918
It's professional wrestlers.

00:11:24.918 --> 00:11:26.710
And it's not because
professional wrestling

00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:30.820
is far and away the most
important thing about people

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:33.340
or the kind of people that
you most need to know about.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:37.000
That's Wikipedia for you.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:38.830
It's a crowdsourced
knowledge base.

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:41.830
So the things we care
more or less about

00:11:41.830 --> 00:11:45.310
get diminished or magnified.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:48.520
It's an imperfect
lens on our world.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:49.440
And that's OK.

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:55.500
But wouldn't it be great to
be able to map out this bias?

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.730
Before we can fight against
our cognitive biases,

00:11:57.730 --> 00:11:59.710
you have to know
even what they are.

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:01.710
How do we get the
tools to do this?

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:05.915
So Michelle and I have
talked about this a lot.

00:12:05.915 --> 00:12:08.040
If you're going to build
a machine learning system,

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.070
it's really helpful to
imagine you're the machine.

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:12.900
That's really the
best first step.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:14.610
Imagine you're the computer.

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:16.510
What is your task?

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:20.070
So let's say that
you're the computer,

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:25.320
and your task is to identify
all of the people who

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:28.980
should be on Wikipedia
and those about whom

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:32.040
we're paying more attention
than the citations deserve.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:33.252
Where would you even begin?

00:12:33.252 --> 00:12:34.710
MICHELLE CASBON:
Well, first, you'd

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:37.350
have to look at all the
existing Wikipedia pages.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:39.300
There are 5 million of them.

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:40.883
JOHN BOHANNON: In English, yes.

00:12:40.883 --> 00:12:42.300
MICHELLE CASBON:
News articles are

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:44.850
being published every day,
so thousands of news articles

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:45.670
on a daily basis.

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:47.003
You'd have to read all of those.

00:12:47.003 --> 00:12:49.350
JOHN BOHANNON: Hundreds of
thousands, in English alone.

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:51.642
MICHELLE CASBON: Come up with
the important information

00:12:51.642 --> 00:12:54.418
in one set that doesn't
exist in the other set--

00:12:54.418 --> 00:12:55.710
kind of impossible for a human.

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:57.390
JOHN BOHANNON: And do the diff.

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:58.640
MICHELLE CASBON: That's a lot.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:03.830
JOHN BOHANNON: So yeah, really,
really sucky task for a human.

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:09.200
We have on order of a million
volunteers trying to do this.

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:12.920
It's the largest such effort
in the world and totally

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:15.110
not keeping up with it.

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:17.250
They've measured the
latency, for example,

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:19.123
when huge things
happen in the world,

00:13:19.123 --> 00:13:20.540
and they're printed
in newspapers.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:22.790
How long does it take before
they arrive on Wikipedia?

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:23.720
It's long.

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:24.950
It takes a long time.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:27.283
Unless it's about professional
wrestling, in which case,

00:13:27.283 --> 00:13:29.310
it's instantaneous.

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:33.210
So this is the kind
of data that we need.

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:35.720
So this is an actual
screenshot of the data

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:39.530
that comes from this
system called Quicksilver.

00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:42.890
So I'm at a startup just up
the street called Primer.

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:47.422
And what this thing is doing
is it's reading all the news

00:13:47.422 --> 00:13:48.630
in a few different languages.

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:51.770
But everything I'm going to talk
about today is about English.

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:55.400
And it also reads
Wikipedia, of course.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:58.370
And what it's doing
is it's extracting

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:01.590
facts about the people and then
writing summaries about them.

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:05.540
And so this is what we need.

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:09.380
So how many of these
people actually

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:12.150
are described in Wikipedia?

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:14.370
So now, what you have
is a ranked list, right?

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:18.080
You can sort how much did we
learn about these people--

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:20.912
in this case, scientists, in
this case, female scientists.

00:14:20.912 --> 00:14:22.370
How much did we
learn automatically

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:25.040
with an AI system from the
news, versus how much is

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:26.830
known about them in Wikipedia?

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:27.890
Ta-da.

00:14:27.890 --> 00:14:29.840
Hardest problem
effectively solved.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:37.100
So when we first started
talking about this,

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:39.600
when we got a lot of coverage
in Wired, for example,

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:41.330
last year, we were--

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.150
we being Primer-- were
really, really proud of this.

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:49.160
Pretty much smug, because we
only had tens of thousands

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:54.140
of people being tracked with on
order of hundreds of thousands

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:55.460
of news docs.

00:14:55.460 --> 00:15:00.920
And we did it from
static JSON ML files,

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:03.230
so really, really not scalable.

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:04.940
And so since then,
we've basically

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:07.310
been getting the
system onto Kubernetes

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:09.690
and trying to make it
actually work at scale.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:12.920
And we immediately started
to run into problems.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:17.787
And Michelle came and
gave us a consultation.

00:15:17.787 --> 00:15:19.370
MICHELLE CASBON:
Yeah, so when I first

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:21.260
got my hands on
Quicksilver, I set out

00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:23.760
to look at the
existing architecture,

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:26.480
figure out how it was
built. It was originally

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:28.040
a set of separate
pieces, running

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:31.700
in a hand-rolled
Kubernetes cluster on AWS.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:34.190
And I had one key
question for John.

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:36.680
As you scale the number of
people you were looking at,

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:38.396
what kind of performance
were you seeing?

00:15:38.396 --> 00:15:39.188
JOHN BOHANNON: Bad.

00:15:39.188 --> 00:15:40.833
[LAUGHS]

00:15:40.833 --> 00:15:42.500
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah,
so it seemed fine

00:15:42.500 --> 00:15:44.300
with just the
first few thousand.

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:48.920
But as you started to
grow to Wikipedia scale,

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:50.160
you ran into challenges.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.490
This is a very familiar problem.

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:56.150
You have this handcrafted,
artisanal ML system

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:58.610
that's been meticulously
sculpted for solving

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:00.340
a tangible problem.

00:16:00.340 --> 00:16:03.830
You have a relational
backend, a document store,

00:16:03.830 --> 00:16:06.440
a caching layer, and a
monolithic coordinates

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:08.640
between all of those pieces.

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:11.090
With that as a starting
point, the first thing we do

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:14.840
is forklift it into GCP.

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:16.850
Some of this was easy.

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:19.700
For example, moving the
Kubernetes manifests onto GKE

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:21.020
was straightforward.

00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:23.050
Thankfully, you were
already running Kubernetes.

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:25.340
And we moved some of those
application config files

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:29.330
into config maps and database
credentials into secrets.

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:31.910
We even moved the document
store from Elastic

00:16:31.910 --> 00:16:33.390
into Cloud Storage.

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.670
Elastic was overkill
for this application.

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:38.450
So we used the simplest
storage we could find.

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:40.922
But other things
were a bit harder.

00:16:40.922 --> 00:16:42.380
When you stand up
your application,

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:43.760
what's the first thing it does?

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:47.300
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah, so step
one, hit postgres and talk

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:48.620
to the knowledge base.

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:50.192
And that's also step n.

00:16:50.192 --> 00:16:52.650
It basically has to iterate
back and forth, back and forth,

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:54.790
back and forth with SQL calls.

00:16:54.790 --> 00:16:57.050
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah,
so I noticed that.

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:00.500
Actually, moving the data
from postgres into Cloud SQL

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:03.530
was just delightfully
straightforward.

00:17:03.530 --> 00:17:05.700
But connecting to it
was another story.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:07.645
So if you haven't
used Cloud SQL before,

00:17:07.645 --> 00:17:09.020
if you want to
run an application

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:11.359
inside of Kubernetes
Engine, you need

00:17:11.359 --> 00:17:13.609
something called a
sidecar that gives you

00:17:13.609 --> 00:17:15.900
a proxy, a connection proxy.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.230
And what it does is it
creates a network tunnel.

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:21.234
It continuously refreshes
your SSL certificate.

00:17:21.234 --> 00:17:22.609
It can be a bit
tricky to set up,

00:17:22.609 --> 00:17:24.359
but once you get it
running, you generally

00:17:24.359 --> 00:17:27.119
don't have to think about it.

00:17:27.119 --> 00:17:32.960
OK, so now that we have
it running in GCP, great.

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:34.098
Now you have two problems.

00:17:34.098 --> 00:17:34.890
JOHN BOHANNON: Yay.

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:36.765
MICHELLE CASBON: Your
application runs poorly

00:17:36.765 --> 00:17:38.020
on two different clouds.

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:40.120
JOHN BOHANNON:
Mission accomplished.

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:43.240
Yeah, so it is still
directionally correct,

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:47.770
though, because although
we're talking about scientists

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:50.440
and Wikipedia as a
motivating example,

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:52.030
we're actually
building products.

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:56.470
So what our customers
want is a system

00:17:56.470 --> 00:17:59.200
that you can just feed in
natural language documents.

00:17:59.200 --> 00:18:01.510
It will automatically
stand up a knowledge base

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:04.510
and update it, as it learns
things from those documents,

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:06.490
and then describe itself
in natural language

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:10.270
ports of some kind, and
always comparing itself

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:11.830
to some source of truth.

00:18:11.830 --> 00:18:14.443
So that's the generalizable
system we're going for.

00:18:14.443 --> 00:18:16.360
And so we want this thing
to be able to deploy

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:19.890
on any kind of cloud
or on-prem, actually.

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:22.670
A lot of customers need
this to work on-prem.

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:25.990
So if we can make it
work in both AWS and GCP,

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:28.420
it's definitely a step
in the right direction.

00:18:28.420 --> 00:18:31.360
MICHELLE CASBON: OK,
so the primary team

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:33.190
did this the hard way.

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:34.830
Sometimes a monolith is OK.

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:36.580
But in your case,
having a design template

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:38.788
would have helped you build
a scalable application

00:18:38.788 --> 00:18:39.580
from the beginning.

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:43.030
JOHN BOHANNON: OK, but just
note that Kubeflow did not exist

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:44.200
when this project started.

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:46.033
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah,
so it's not too late.

00:18:46.033 --> 00:18:47.420
Kubeflow can still help.

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:49.260
So let's look at how.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.620
I prepared a
recording for today.

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:54.100
What we'll be doing is
spinning up clusters

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:56.140
and watching nodes
scale up and down

00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:58.240
and performing training,
which is about as

00:18:58.240 --> 00:18:59.830
exciting as watching paint dry.

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:02.320
So we'll skip over some
of those boring parts,

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:09.280
and we'll start by looking
at our empty GCP console.

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:12.850
So from a blank slate,
the question is always,

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:13.990
where do you begin?

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:15.940
Primer was brand new to GCP.

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:19.120
So we started with an empty
project, and what we need

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:22.430
is a way to create a fresh
cluster from scratch.

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:23.100
Well, good news.

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:24.706
There's an app for that.

00:19:24.706 --> 00:19:26.620
It's called Click to Deploy.

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:29.560
And if you go to
deploy.kubeflow.cloud,

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:31.570
you'll find this form.

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:33.830
We need a couple of
pieces of information.

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:36.190
You have a project name.

00:19:36.190 --> 00:19:37.970
We need to give our
deployment a name.

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:39.040
Let's call it Kubey.

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:40.052
JOHN BOHANNON: Let's.

00:19:40.052 --> 00:19:42.010
MICHELLE CASBON: Give it
our OAuth credentials.

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:43.468
If you're not sure
to create those,

00:19:43.468 --> 00:19:45.770
there are instructions
on the left.

00:19:45.770 --> 00:19:46.390
And that's it.

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:49.320
We're using the very
latest version of Kubeflow.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:51.980
It was just released last week.

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:56.240
So how many people here
have used Kubeflow before?

00:19:56.240 --> 00:20:01.410
OK, of those, who
installed it themselves?

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:04.440
OK, all right, one person.

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:07.080
So you understand
exactly how hard this is.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.210
This application is
getting rid of all

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:10.530
of that hard work for you.

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:14.310
What it's doing is
spinning up a GKE cluster.

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:16.830
It's setting up the project,
creating service accounts,

00:20:16.830 --> 00:20:21.420
enabling APIs, and installing
all the Kubeflow components.

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:23.370
And you can watch those install.

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:26.370
It takes about five minutes
to get to this point,

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:29.070
where you have
everything running.

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:31.140
But also, it gives
you a public URL.

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:33.782
That takes about another
10 to 15 minutes.

00:20:33.782 --> 00:20:35.240
And once that's up
and running, you

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:38.700
can go into your Services tab,
find your ingress service,

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:42.600
and just click on the
link that it gives you.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:45.200
So what you have now, this
is your central dashboard,

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:48.680
and this is the
entry point into all

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:51.350
of the sort of Kubeflow UIs.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:54.110
Well, that's the
official installation.

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:56.450
That's what you get if
you just use the app.

00:20:56.450 --> 00:21:00.210
But I always do one extra
little step for my clusters.

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:02.910
I'm going to show you the sort
of secret backdoor feature.

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:05.540
It's one of my
favorite GCP features.

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:09.460
It's called autoprovisioning,
and it's a GKE beta feature.

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:12.380
So if you run this command,
you can specify maximum limits

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:14.960
for CPU and memory.

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:19.050
And what it does is when you
run jobs on your cluster,

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:20.780
it will scale the
number of nodes

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:23.450
up and down, based on the
requirements that are there.

00:21:23.450 --> 00:21:26.690
So this takes about
five minutes or so.

00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:28.610
But once we have
it up and running,

00:21:28.610 --> 00:21:32.218
we don't have to think about
the size of our cluster.

00:21:32.218 --> 00:21:34.010
So if we take a look
at the number of nodes

00:21:34.010 --> 00:21:36.830
that we start out with, we've
essentially created a baseline.

00:21:36.830 --> 00:21:38.930
We have a node pool
with two nodes, which

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:40.850
is enough to run all
the base components

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:43.100
and a few extra jobs.

00:21:43.100 --> 00:21:47.995
So we're starting off
with just these two nodes.

00:21:47.995 --> 00:21:49.980
OK.

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:55.180
So now that we have our
running installation,

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:57.560
what exactly does that mean?

00:21:57.560 --> 00:21:59.470
So we just saw at
deploy.kubeflow.cloud--

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:02.950
if you want to try it
yourself, you can go there--

00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:05.020
we had a number of
different components.

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:07.932
I'm just going to refer
to them as ingress.

00:22:07.932 --> 00:22:09.640
There's a lot going
on behind the scenes.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:11.650
We'll talk a little
bit about it later.

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:14.000
But ingress essentially
gives us that public IP.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:16.270
And it's a single ingestion
point into our cluster.

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:19.240
It handles all of the auth, so
that nothing inside the cluster

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:21.520
has to think about it.

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:23.230
From there, we saw
the central dashboard.

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:25.960
And that gives us access to
all the other dashboards.

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:30.640
We have some for training,
some for running our pipelines.

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:33.520
Argo is the workflow engine
that powers pipelines.

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:37.150
Katib is a way of performing
hyperparameter tuning.

00:22:37.150 --> 00:22:40.110
And then we also
have a notebook hub.

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:43.020
We have operators for running
training jobs in a distributed

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:44.790
way.

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:46.440
Argo helps us run our pipelines.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:49.340
Katib helps us with
hyperparameter tuning.

00:22:49.340 --> 00:22:52.050
And once we have our trained
models, we can serve them.

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:54.120
We have a few components
that help with that--

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:56.730
TensorFlow for TensorFlow
models and Seldon

00:22:56.730 --> 00:23:00.530
for any Python model.

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:03.950
So at this point, we have
a blank Kubeflow slate.

00:23:03.950 --> 00:23:06.810
Now how can I take an existing
application, Quicksilver,

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:09.760
and run it in Kubeflow?

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:11.350
So we can take your
existing manifests

00:23:11.350 --> 00:23:16.547
and apply them to our cluster,
but not from a terminal.

00:23:16.547 --> 00:23:18.880
What's the language that your
application is written in?

00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:19.797
JOHN BOHANNON: Python.

00:23:19.797 --> 00:23:21.463
MICHELLE CASBON: And
what about the rest

00:23:21.463 --> 00:23:22.810
of the code base at Primer?

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:23.800
JOHN BOHANNON:
Pretty much Python.

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:25.000
MICHELLE CASBON:
It's all Python.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:26.480
So you have all this
Python expertise.

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:27.010
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah.

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:28.427
MICHELLE CASBON:
Let's use Python.

00:23:28.427 --> 00:23:29.290
JOHN BOHANNON: Yay.

00:23:29.290 --> 00:23:29.710
MICHELLE CASBON:
So this is what it

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:31.502
looks like to run our
monolith on Kubeflow.

00:23:31.502 --> 00:23:36.280
We create a pipeline, and each
step is one of our components.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:40.493
Let's see it in action and run
our monolith as a pipeline.

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:46.420
So start by going to
the pipelines dashboard,

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:47.860
and there's nothing there yet.

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:53.277
So we can upload a file and
create a pipeline using Python.

00:23:53.277 --> 00:23:54.610
We have a few Python files here.

00:23:54.610 --> 00:23:57.340
We'll look at the
monolith version.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:59.650
And we start off by
importing our libraries

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:02.510
and defining the image
that we want to use.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:04.960
And we set up two
distinct steps,

00:24:04.960 --> 00:24:06.700
and we compile those
into a pipeline.

00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:09.060
We're using this
pipeline decorator,

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:13.840
and we set up each step in the
order we want them to run in.

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:16.090
And then those
last three lines is

00:24:16.090 --> 00:24:18.960
what compiles it
into an archive file

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:22.050
that we can then upload
into our cluster.

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:26.060
So we have our caching
layer, our monolith step,

00:24:26.060 --> 00:24:28.013
and if we just compile
it as is, we'll

00:24:28.013 --> 00:24:31.560
get a file that we
can bring into our UI.

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:34.960
What's inside that file, this
is Argo workflow definition.

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:36.937
So it's really
detailed and long.

00:24:36.937 --> 00:24:38.020
You can open up that file.

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:38.603
It's all text.

00:24:38.603 --> 00:24:39.936
It's not binary.

00:24:39.936 --> 00:24:43.980
But you can upload
it directly as is.

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:46.050
JOHN BOHANNON: And you
didn't have to write it.

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:47.925
MICHELLE CASBON: You
didn't have to write it.

00:24:47.925 --> 00:24:49.270
You could use the Pipelines SDK.

00:24:51.820 --> 00:24:54.420
So if we upload it, we can
see a visual representation

00:24:54.420 --> 00:24:58.470
with two steps, caching
and then our application.

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:02.608
And if we want to run
it, we'll give it a name.

00:25:02.608 --> 00:25:04.650
And this will actually
execute it on our cluster.

00:25:04.650 --> 00:25:07.870
So we can view it
executing from this UI,

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:10.740
including accessing
all the logs.

00:25:10.740 --> 00:25:13.490
We'll see what that looks like.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:15.980
So our redis container,
it's pulling the image.

00:25:15.980 --> 00:25:19.050
It's creating it.

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:22.560
And then it sets
up our application.

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:24.320
JOHN BOHANNON: This
is my favorite part.

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:27.828
I think this UI is
really beautiful.

00:25:27.828 --> 00:25:29.370
MICHELLE CASBON: So
there we have it.

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:31.520
Oh, this is the bare minimum.

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:34.750
So at this point, Quicksilver
can handle daily workloads

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:37.040
involving scientists.

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:39.340
But for a generalizable
solution, what kind of scale

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:40.330
do you need?

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:42.160
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah,
so, I mean, if we're

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:46.180
going to power all of
Wikipedia editing for anyone

00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:49.150
in the world who wants to track
some entity, some Wikipedia

00:25:49.150 --> 00:25:52.120
page, and have the
news automatically

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:55.480
be processed and give them
updates on what's new,

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:57.438
then definitely, we're
talking about web scale.

00:25:57.438 --> 00:25:59.480
MICHELLE CASBON: So there's
no getting around it.

00:25:59.480 --> 00:26:01.058
I hate to break
this to you, but you

00:26:01.058 --> 00:26:02.350
need to carve up your monolith.

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:04.515
JOHN BOHANNON: No, don't
carve up my monolith.

00:26:04.515 --> 00:26:05.890
MICHELLE CASBON:
So this is where

00:26:05.890 --> 00:26:08.710
Kubeflow components can help.

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:09.930
This is what that looks like.

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:11.520
It's a longer pipeline now.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:14.027
It's split up into
smaller pieces.

00:26:14.027 --> 00:26:15.360
You have a data ingestion phase.

00:26:15.360 --> 00:26:18.180
And once your data is there,
you can begin training.

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:20.590
And you can do that in
parallel, separately.

00:26:20.590 --> 00:26:24.390
Once that training is complete,
you can serve those models,

00:26:24.390 --> 00:26:27.650
and then stand up your web app.

00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:29.350
Well, that's the concept.

00:26:29.350 --> 00:26:32.760
How do we actually
implement that?

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:34.560
Let's kick it up a notch.

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:38.340
How can I take an existing
pipeline and make it better?

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:42.090
OK, audience question-- what's
the best tool for quickly

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:45.388
iterating on a Python file?

00:26:45.388 --> 00:26:46.930
What are some of
your favorite tools?

00:26:46.930 --> 00:26:48.586
AUDIENCE: Jupyter.

00:26:48.586 --> 00:26:51.880
MICHELLE CASBON: Jupyter
notebooks, excellent answer.

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:53.768
JOHN BOHANNON: [LAUGHS]

00:26:53.768 --> 00:26:56.484
MICHELLE CASBON: All right.

00:26:56.484 --> 00:26:57.442
Where did the mouse go?

00:27:00.304 --> 00:27:01.135
OK.

00:27:01.135 --> 00:27:03.010
So we'll start by going
to the Notebooks tab.

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:04.980
You read my mind.

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:08.200
OK, so what's better than
hosting your notebooks locally

00:27:08.200 --> 00:27:10.300
and competing with all
of your open browser tabs

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:11.170
for resources?

00:27:11.170 --> 00:27:12.730
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:12.730 --> 00:27:15.190
MICHELLE CASBON: Running it
on someone else's resources,

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:15.760
that's right.

00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:18.550
That way, you have room
for all the cat memes.

00:27:18.550 --> 00:27:22.720
So what we're doing here is
Kubeflow provides this spawner,

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:25.440
and we can spin up our very
own JupyterHub instance.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:27.858
This takes about two
or three minutes.

00:27:27.858 --> 00:27:29.650
But this is all running
inside our cluster.

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:31.270
So we can access it locally.

00:27:31.270 --> 00:27:35.940
We connect right here, and
we have our own instance.

00:27:35.940 --> 00:27:40.750
So we'll go and we'll find
our microservices notebook.

00:27:40.750 --> 00:27:43.710
Upload our own file.

00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:47.620
JOHN BOHANNON: It is also really
great for reproducibility.

00:27:47.620 --> 00:27:49.330
I can point you at
the exact notebook

00:27:49.330 --> 00:27:50.560
where I ran everything.

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:52.636
You can see how I got it wrong.

00:27:52.636 --> 00:27:55.440
MICHELLE CASBON: OK, so,
fun fact about notebooks.

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:57.510
The very first time
I saw a notebook,

00:27:57.510 --> 00:27:59.070
I was in Paco's workshop.

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:01.560
This was probably 2013, 2014.

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:03.880
I don't even know
if he knows this,

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:06.360
but I learned everything I
know about notebooks from Paco.

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:07.527
That was the very beginning.

00:28:07.527 --> 00:28:10.480
So you're seeing
this in action here.

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:14.215
OK, so this is our microservices
version of Quicksilver.

00:28:14.215 --> 00:28:15.590
The first thing
we do, we're just

00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:17.410
using a stock TensorFlow image.

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:19.560
So we're going to install
Kubeflow pipelines

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:24.540
on top of it, and then we
import those libraries.

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:27.930
We define some static variables,
so which images we want to run,

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:29.325
which versions.

00:28:29.325 --> 00:28:31.200
And then we retrieve
our database credentials

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:32.040
from secrets.

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:36.110
That way, nothing is
hardcoded in GitHub anywhere.

00:28:36.110 --> 00:28:38.320
And then we define
each of our steps.

00:28:38.320 --> 00:28:41.548
We ingest our data
first, and then we train.

00:28:41.548 --> 00:28:43.090
And this step is
kind of interesting.

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:45.310
It's doing some unique things.

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:47.590
So we're making
requests for resources.

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:50.350
We're saying that we want
a couple of CPU cores

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:53.170
and 32 gigs of RAM.

00:28:53.170 --> 00:28:55.353
We're grabbing those
database credentials,

00:28:55.353 --> 00:28:56.770
and then we're
adding our sidecar.

00:28:56.770 --> 00:29:00.370
This is what gives us the
connection to Cloud SQL.

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:02.260
This was a brand new feature.

00:29:02.260 --> 00:29:05.020
Thank you to [INAUDIBLE]
for committing that

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:07.990
two weeks ago, just in time
for me to get this prepared.

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:09.820
This is one of my
favorite things.

00:29:09.820 --> 00:29:13.030
So this training step, because
we're requesting resources just

00:29:13.030 --> 00:29:16.570
for this one step, that
means that we don't need it

00:29:16.570 --> 00:29:18.140
for the entire pipeline.

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:20.980
So being able to specify
it at a step level

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:24.690
is what helps us to
scale at our cluster.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:26.280
So we compose our pipeline.

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:28.350
We define each of those
steps and the order

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:30.000
we want them to run in.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.457
We generate our archive file
right here in the notebook,

00:29:32.457 --> 00:29:34.540
and we can actually kick
it off from here as well.

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:38.770
So we have a link that brings
us directly to our Pipelines

00:29:38.770 --> 00:29:39.640
dashboard.

00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:41.760
And we can see our
pipeline running.

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:43.250
JOHN BOHANNON: Ta-da.

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:45.600
MICHELLE CASBON: So our data
ingestion ran just fine.

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:47.497
But our training job--

00:29:47.497 --> 00:29:48.330
I know that's small.

00:29:48.330 --> 00:29:50.310
It says this step
is impending state.

00:29:50.310 --> 00:29:52.362
It's unschedulable,
insufficient memory.

00:29:52.362 --> 00:29:53.112
JOHN BOHANNON: No.

00:29:53.112 --> 00:29:54.890
Call DevOps.

00:29:54.890 --> 00:29:57.010
MICHELLE CASBON: OK,
so what do we do?

00:29:57.010 --> 00:29:58.390
Let's do some troubleshooting.

00:29:58.390 --> 00:30:01.210
We'll drop down to
the container level.

00:30:01.210 --> 00:30:04.340
Let's see what's going
on with our training pod.

00:30:04.340 --> 00:30:07.050
We have a few more
details in here.

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:09.620
And it looks like, OK, so
we couldn't schedule it,

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:12.010
but TriggeredScaleUp.

00:30:12.010 --> 00:30:12.890
This is a good sign.

00:30:12.890 --> 00:30:15.610
This means that our
autoprovisioning is kicking in.

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:17.680
And because we only had
two nodes originally,

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:20.570
and we wanted more RAM
than was available,

00:30:20.570 --> 00:30:22.180
we can see this happening.

00:30:22.180 --> 00:30:25.575
If we go into the console, we
can see the nodes spinning up.

00:30:25.575 --> 00:30:27.200
JOHN BOHANNON: DevOps
is already on it.

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:28.630
Didn't even have to call them.

00:30:28.630 --> 00:30:29.130
Oh, DevOps.

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:31.870
MICHELLE CASBON: All via Python.

00:30:31.870 --> 00:30:34.960
So that all happens
in the background.

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:36.940
And as soon as that
node becomes available,

00:30:36.940 --> 00:30:38.740
that'll take a few minutes.

00:30:38.740 --> 00:30:40.420
Our job will complete.

00:30:40.420 --> 00:30:44.710
And then the rest of our
pipeline can finish running.

00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:47.500
Because what's better-- having
a fact cluster up all the time

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:51.040
or having a fact cluster
up just for a few hours?

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:53.980
OK, so the cool thing is that
once that pipeline is done,

00:30:53.980 --> 00:30:55.930
the nodes disappear.

00:30:55.930 --> 00:30:59.140
So the node pool that was
stood up gets deleted.

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:03.080
And our application
is up and running.

00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:07.120
OK, so I talked about
the ingress components.

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:10.040
And I told you there was kind
of a lot going on behind it.

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:13.450
One of the cool things about it
is that there's a reverse HTTP

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:14.240
proxy.

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:18.550
So we can just add a
route to our external URL

00:31:18.550 --> 00:31:21.250
and navigate to
slash Quicksilver.

00:31:21.250 --> 00:31:24.567
And we can actually see our
application up and running.

00:31:24.567 --> 00:31:25.900
JOHN BOHANNON: Yay, there it is.

00:31:28.666 --> 00:31:31.440
MICHELLE CASBON: All right.

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:34.720
So what has Kubeflow given us?

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:36.550
We've gotten portability,
so that we can

00:31:36.550 --> 00:31:39.130
run on any Kubernetes cluster.

00:31:39.130 --> 00:31:41.770
Kubeflow's entirely open source,
and it uses vanilla Kubernetes

00:31:41.770 --> 00:31:42.640
APIs.

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:45.400
So as long as you're running
open source Kubernetes,

00:31:45.400 --> 00:31:47.230
you can run Kubeflow on top.

00:31:47.230 --> 00:31:51.220
That means you can run it
on another cloud, on-prem,

00:31:51.220 --> 00:31:52.300
or even local.

00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:54.620
There's no vendor lock in.

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:56.610
We've also gotten scalability.

00:31:56.610 --> 00:31:59.680
So that means that our costs
can grow in line with our user

00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:02.477
growth, and we can be very
fine grained about that.

00:32:02.477 --> 00:32:04.060
And then we also
have composed ability

00:32:04.060 --> 00:32:07.526
so that our ML design
can handle web scale.

00:32:07.526 --> 00:32:10.340
As platforms like Kubeflow
make it easier and easier

00:32:10.340 --> 00:32:13.370
to build ML applications,
it's our responsibility

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:15.685
to build ethical ML systems.

00:32:15.685 --> 00:32:20.830
A revealing bias can have a
positive effect immediately.

00:32:20.830 --> 00:32:23.110
Now that we have our
system up and running,

00:32:23.110 --> 00:32:25.660
what can we expect to happen
when we ask Quicksilver what it

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:27.390
knows about a fellow speaker?

00:32:27.390 --> 00:32:30.400
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah, so when
I saw that login screen,

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:35.170
by the way, just like weeks ago,
literally, I was very excited.

00:32:35.170 --> 00:32:40.810
And to test it out, we have been
actually taking the speakers

00:32:40.810 --> 00:32:43.263
of this very event--

00:32:43.263 --> 00:32:45.430
just little bits of information
about them, really--

00:32:45.430 --> 00:32:48.520
just like name, employer--

00:32:48.520 --> 00:32:51.370
as tuples, keywords
if you have them.

00:32:51.370 --> 00:32:54.100
But really, it works just
with name and employer.

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:59.140
And feed them into the system,
and it reads all the news

00:32:59.140 --> 00:33:00.640
for the past several
years at least.

00:33:00.640 --> 00:33:03.760
And it tries to find
everything it can about them

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:05.500
and assemble together a profile.

00:33:05.500 --> 00:33:07.000
And then it hits
Wikipedia, and it's

00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:09.220
like, well, how much
do I know compared

00:33:09.220 --> 00:33:11.320
to what Wikipedia knows?

00:33:11.320 --> 00:33:15.700
And so yeah, we
actually, we found

00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:21.480
someone who popped right to
the top when we ran this.

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:28.300
So she had been pretty quiet
in the news until mid-March.

00:33:28.300 --> 00:33:32.200
March 14, in fact, is
when things just exploded.

00:33:32.200 --> 00:33:34.740
And anyone know the
significance of March 14?

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:38.910
Pi Day, yes.

00:33:38.910 --> 00:33:42.090
So there is an
engineer at Google,

00:33:42.090 --> 00:33:46.650
named Emma, who calculated
31.4 trillion digits of pi,

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:49.410
breaking the world
record very easily.

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:53.130
And there was just a ton
of news coverage of this.

00:33:53.130 --> 00:33:54.720
She did it on GCP.

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:57.030
It was a cloud computing feat.

00:33:57.030 --> 00:33:59.790
And sure enough, as
journalists basically

00:33:59.790 --> 00:34:05.130
did their job of describing
this scientific feat

00:34:05.130 --> 00:34:06.990
and describing the
scientist behind it,

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:09.239
you started to learn
more and more about Emma.

00:34:09.239 --> 00:34:12.179
Lots of little bits of string
that the AI system gathered

00:34:12.179 --> 00:34:15.750
together in order to have
a bunch of cool things

00:34:15.750 --> 00:34:16.882
to say about someone.

00:34:16.882 --> 00:34:18.340
Now, this was
trained on Wikipedia.

00:34:18.340 --> 00:34:20.780
So it's essentially
like, its model

00:34:20.780 --> 00:34:24.909
of what counts as interesting
is everyone on Wikipedia.

00:34:24.909 --> 00:34:27.900
So it's just finding facts
that kind of basically

00:34:27.900 --> 00:34:30.570
are similar to that
and assembling them.

00:34:30.570 --> 00:34:33.570
And can we run it?

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:39.030
Yeah, so this is us
actually getting her profile

00:34:39.030 --> 00:34:40.500
from Quicksilver.

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:43.199
And ta-da she is at the top.

00:34:43.199 --> 00:34:47.340
And this is what the
machine just like spat out.

00:34:47.340 --> 00:34:48.300
Hey, there's Emma.

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:49.389
I know about her.

00:34:49.389 --> 00:34:54.570
And if you have the job
of keeping Wikipedia up

00:34:54.570 --> 00:34:59.100
to date with the latest
people in the world of science

00:34:59.100 --> 00:35:02.340
and what they're up to and the
discoveries they're making,

00:35:02.340 --> 00:35:03.960
this is the hardest step.

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:06.277
How would you even
have found Emma?

00:35:06.277 --> 00:35:08.610
Unless you were literally
reading all the news every day

00:35:08.610 --> 00:35:11.550
and just keeping track of every
single scientist ever mentioned

00:35:11.550 --> 00:35:14.820
and then visiting Wikipedia
to find out what you missed,

00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:16.690
that is like an impossible task.

00:35:16.690 --> 00:35:21.780
So today, actually, as
of early this morning,

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:25.440
at night, this is now public.

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:28.450
So all of you can go
to that URL at the top,

00:35:28.450 --> 00:35:34.740
quicksilver.primer.ai, and we
are just updating this everyday

00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:35.880
automatically.

00:35:35.880 --> 00:35:40.110
And so once upon a time,
what we were doing was,

00:35:40.110 --> 00:35:42.960
I would generate these
spreadsheets of data

00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:44.970
about scientists
using Quicksilver.

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:48.987
And then I would help
people who run editathons.

00:35:48.987 --> 00:35:50.820
So that's basically,
you get a bunch people,

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:52.230
and you give them
pizza and beer,

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:54.240
and you edit
Wikipedia as quickly

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:56.190
as you can to improve it.

00:35:56.190 --> 00:35:57.300
It's an editathon.

00:35:57.300 --> 00:35:58.863
And it's really,
really hard work.

00:35:58.863 --> 00:36:00.030
It's surprisingly hard work.

00:36:00.030 --> 00:36:02.730
And just those
spreadsheets of data output

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:04.107
were incredibly useful.

00:36:04.107 --> 00:36:05.190
That's what everyone said.

00:36:05.190 --> 00:36:09.540
We ran them in New York,
London, Washington, DC.

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:12.130
And the feedback was
just very, very positive.

00:36:12.130 --> 00:36:14.190
This kind of data
is what we need.

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:18.180
And then last week, we
actually had a editathon

00:36:18.180 --> 00:36:22.560
just down the street, using
these completely generated

00:36:22.560 --> 00:36:23.138
profiles.

00:36:23.138 --> 00:36:24.930
Because one of the
pieces of feedback I got

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:28.650
was those spreadsheets of
data about people is handy,

00:36:28.650 --> 00:36:32.580
but it's still
pretty hard to read.

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:34.140
What we really need
is to just have

00:36:34.140 --> 00:36:36.690
the computer write the first
draft of the Wikipedia article.

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:39.220
Can you do that?

00:36:39.220 --> 00:36:40.320
So we did it.

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:43.470
Now, essentially, you can
go here every single day,

00:36:43.470 --> 00:36:47.610
and it will just
find people that it

00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:50.430
has detected as having
lots of information

00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:54.060
in public, citable sources,
where that information seems

00:36:54.060 --> 00:36:55.600
to be missing from Wikipedia.

00:36:55.600 --> 00:36:58.560
So now, anyone,
anywhere, anytime

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:01.920
has that first really hard
cognitive bias problem

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:05.040
of what to pay attention to
in the first place solved,

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:08.080
at least for scientists.

00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:10.280
MICHELLE CASBON: OK, so
is Emma on this list?

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:11.300
Does she have a page?

00:37:11.300 --> 00:37:13.758
JOHN BOHANNON: Yeah, so I'm
very proud to say that Emma now

00:37:13.758 --> 00:37:15.240
has a Wikipedia page.

00:37:15.240 --> 00:37:16.730
And this is a good one.

00:37:16.730 --> 00:37:19.160
So you remember I showed
you that Wikipedia page

00:37:19.160 --> 00:37:21.680
of the scientist from Harvard?

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:23.420
It's kind of modest, no image.

00:37:26.750 --> 00:37:27.980
This is a better page.

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:31.230
It's actually got more sources.

00:37:31.230 --> 00:37:33.080
It's really well interlinked.

00:37:33.080 --> 00:37:37.910
All those blue letters
are basically Wiki links,

00:37:37.910 --> 00:37:41.300
embedding this page within
Wikipedia's Knowledge Graph.

00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:43.420
That interlinking
is really important.

00:37:43.420 --> 00:37:44.870
So this is a good page.

00:37:44.870 --> 00:37:46.730
However, I kid you not.

00:37:46.730 --> 00:37:49.892
Within two days of this
page getting created--

00:37:49.892 --> 00:37:51.350
which, by the way,
was made by Jess

00:37:51.350 --> 00:37:55.070
Wade, all credit due to her,
a collaborator and friend

00:37:55.070 --> 00:37:55.850
based in the UK--

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:03.980
this started to appear in the
talk page of Emma's Wikipedia

00:38:03.980 --> 00:38:06.090
page.

00:38:06.090 --> 00:38:09.510
MICHELLE CASBON: OK, let's zoom
in on the logic behind that.

00:38:09.510 --> 00:38:12.770
JOHN BOHANNON: This is how
the door gets cracked open.

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:16.070
This is the very beginning of
the process of deleting someone

00:38:16.070 --> 00:38:17.403
from Wikipedia.

00:38:17.403 --> 00:38:18.820
MICHELLE CASBON:
OK, so the reason

00:38:18.820 --> 00:38:20.440
this person gave for
wanting to delete

00:38:20.440 --> 00:38:25.300
Emma's page is they say that
the computation may be notable,

00:38:25.300 --> 00:38:27.190
but that is not enough
by itself to make

00:38:27.190 --> 00:38:29.628
the person who did it notable.

00:38:29.628 --> 00:38:31.670
JOHN BOHANNON: So it's a
bit of a paradox, right?

00:38:31.670 --> 00:38:34.560
If all you're famous for
is winning the Nobel Prize,

00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:38.380
like really just the
one thing, it just

00:38:38.380 --> 00:38:40.900
doesn't quite make the cut.

00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:43.750
And again, these
are not bad actors.

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:48.640
These are just people, one of
the many, many volunteers just

00:38:48.640 --> 00:38:50.590
trying to keep Wikipedia's
standards high,

00:38:50.590 --> 00:38:55.750
but it's a hard, subjective task
to decide what the world should

00:38:55.750 --> 00:38:58.430
and should not know about.

00:38:58.430 --> 00:39:00.560
MICHELLE CASBON: Paco,
please join us on stage.

00:39:00.560 --> 00:39:03.740
JOHN BOHANNON: So we're going
to do something with you

00:39:03.740 --> 00:39:07.730
here now on this public
stage at a public event.

00:39:10.420 --> 00:39:14.650
We're going to introduce to
you the first ever Notability

00:39:14.650 --> 00:39:15.790
Award.

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:17.050
We call it the Kubey.

00:39:17.050 --> 00:39:18.553
MICHELLE CASBON: A Kubey.

00:39:18.553 --> 00:39:20.470
JOHN BOHANNON: And it
is given to a person who

00:39:20.470 --> 00:39:23.260
has abundant, significant
information in public citable

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:26.200
sources about their
life and work.

00:39:26.200 --> 00:39:31.840
It's a computationally
generated prize.

00:39:31.840 --> 00:39:35.500
I think AI can start
giving prizes to humans

00:39:35.500 --> 00:39:37.300
that it learns things about.

00:39:37.300 --> 00:39:42.535
And the first ever Kubey--

00:39:42.535 --> 00:39:43.910
PACO NATHAN: Thank
you very much.

00:39:43.910 --> 00:39:45.310
JOHN BOHANNON: Goes to-- wait.

00:39:45.310 --> 00:39:46.352
We don't know who won it.

00:39:48.220 --> 00:39:49.510
No, it's not going to him.

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:52.730
MICHELLE CASBON: The very
first recipient of this award

00:39:52.730 --> 00:39:56.020
is sitting amongst us today
right here in the audience.

00:39:56.020 --> 00:39:56.850
Who could it be?

00:39:56.850 --> 00:39:58.130
PACO NATHAN: So let
me open this up here.

00:39:58.130 --> 00:39:58.670
JOHN BOHANNON: One of you.

00:39:58.670 --> 00:39:58.840
MICHELLE CASBON: One of you.

00:39:58.840 --> 00:39:59.673
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:39:59.673 --> 00:40:01.840
MICHELLE CASBON: We will
invite you up to the stage.

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:03.850
PACO NATHAN: OK, I'm
going to rip this open.

00:40:03.850 --> 00:40:06.185
This is a very exciting moment.

00:40:06.185 --> 00:40:11.390
The first ever Notability Award
goes to the one, the only Emma

00:40:11.390 --> 00:40:12.840
Haruka Iwao.

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:14.090
Please join us in [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:14.090 --> 00:40:15.260
Emma, come on up.

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:16.160
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:16.160 --> 00:40:20.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

